WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.520
System scope is the foundation for evidence.

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:07.220
The cloud service offering must be described clearly enough that reviewers understand what

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.400
is included,

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:12.780
what is excluded, which environments matter, which dependencies support the service,

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.560
and where customer responsibilities begin.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:18.900
Boundary documentation is not just a compliance diagram.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.520
It determines which controls need evidence and which systems are relevant to the risk

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:24.620
decision.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:29.160
A practical boundary description includes application components, infrastructure,

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:36.260
data stores, management interfaces, identity systems, network paths, external services,

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.680
support tooling, build and deployment processes, and monitoring systems as appropriate.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.820
It should also describe data flows, administrative access paths,

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:49.400
customer-managed settings, inherited platform controls,

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:54.340
and dependencies that affect confidentiality, integrity, or availability.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.960
Scope should be visible in words and diagrams.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:00.300
Included and excluded components need careful treatment.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:02.280
If a component is excluded,

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:06.740
the documentation should make clear why it is outside the cloud service offering and

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.800
whether it still affects the service.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:11.100
If an external service is relied upon,

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:15.960
the team should explain the relationship and any inherited or shared control assumptions.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:20.900
Ambiguity creates gaps, especially when multiple teams assume someone else is responsible.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.240
Unclear scope creates assessment and operational risk.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.860
Evidence may be collected for the wrong systems.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.240
Control narratives may overstate coverage.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.860
Customer responsibilities may be misunderstood.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.360
Vulnerabilities or incidents may be routed to the wrong owner.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:39.820
A clear authorization boundary helps assessors, reviewers, engineers,

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:44.840
and operations teams evaluate the service consistently and act on the same understanding

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.420
of what is in scope.
